Sections "STUDY" contain missmatched items: {'STUDY_SUMMARY': ["The heart’s primary fuels are glucose and fatty acids, which are maintained in a reciprocal balance by the pyruvate dehydrogenase kinases (PDK) enzymes/pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (PDH) axis. During fasted conditions, PDK activity is increased to phosphorylate PDH and inhibit the oxidation of pyruvate, consequently increasing mitochondrial fatty acid utilization. Although PDK1 is highly abundant in the heart, the specific contribution of PDK1 to this effect on lipid metabolism was unclear. So, we knocked down PDK1 in H9c2 cells cardiomyocytes to delineate this role. Our preliminary experiments revealed a decrease in mitochondria fatty acid utilization in palmitate-treated PDK1 knock down cells compared to controls cells. However, the exact mechanism behind this phenotype remained unclear. So, we designed a time series study to delineate the mechanism behind PDK1's action. Briefly, PDK1 knockdown and control H9c2 cells were first differentiated into a cardiomyocyte phenotype then labelled with 13C16 palmitate after which cells were transferred to nutrient-depleted media to model fasting. Parallel experiments were performed with regular palmitate conjugated to BSA. Lipidomics analysis was performed on collected cells.", "The heart’s primary fuels are glucose and fatty acids, which are maintained in a reciprocal balance by the pyruvate dehydrogenase kinases (PDK) enzymes/pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (PDH) axis. During fasted conditions, PDK activity is increased to phosphorylate PDH and inhibit the oxidation of pyruvate, consequently increasing mitochondrial fatty acid utilization. Although PDK1 is highly abundant in the heart, the specific contribution of PDK1 to this effect on lipid metabolism was unclear. So, we knocked down PDK1 in H9c2 cells cardiomyocytes to delineate this role. Our preliminary experiments revealed a decrease in mitochondria fatty acid utilization in palmitate-treated PDK1 knock down cells compared to controls cells. However, the exact mechanism behind this phenotype remained unclear. So, we designed a time series study to delineate the mechanism behind PDK1''s action. Briefly, PDK1 knockdown and control H9c2 cells were first differentiated into a cardiomyocyte phenotype then labelled with 13C16 palmitate after which cells were transferred to nutrient-depleted media to model fasting. Parallel experiments were performed with regular palmitate conjugated to BSA. Lipidomics analysis was performed on collected cells."]}
